<KkShell
  title="Local SEO Agency (Preview)"
  description="Local SEO for the businesses that want the map pack calls, in the Dead River look."
  kicker="LOCAL SEO"
  headline="WHEN THEY SEARCH &ldquo;NEAR ME,&rdquo; THREE BUSINESSES GET THE CALL. BE ONE OF THE THREE."
  sub="The map with three businesses on it gets most of the clicks. Local SEO is how you get in it, and stay in it."
  faqTitle="LOCAL SEO, STRAIGHT ANSWERS"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p class="kk-dear">Hey,</p>
  <p>Do a search right now. Your trade, plus your city. Or your trade plus "near me."</p>
  <p>Before the ads, before the websites, there's a map with three businesses on it. That's where most of the calls go. People don't scroll past it. They pick one of the three and dial.</p>
  <p>If you're not in it, you don't exist to that person. They call whoever is.</p>
  <p>And that click didn't cost you a dollar. Which is why it's the most valuable click in marketing for a local business.</p>
  <h2>WHY YOU'RE NOT IN IT</h2>
  <p>We've pulled up hundreds of these profiles with owners, and it's almost always the same three things.</p>
  <p><strong>A half-finished profile.</strong> Wrong category, half the services missing, three photos from 2019, no posts. Google reads that as "probably not the best answer" and shows somebody else.</p>
  <p><strong>Too few reviews.</strong> Not zero. Just fewer than the guy in the map spot, and none from this month. People compare the stars and the count before they call anybody. So does Google.</p>
  <p><strong>Listings that disagree.</strong> Your phone number is one thing on your site, another on Yelp, your hours are wrong on Apple Maps, and there's an old address from three years ago still floating around. Google trusts you less every time it finds a mismatch.</p>
  <p>None of that is hard to fix. It's just that nobody's doing it.</p>
  <h2>WHAT WE DO</h2>
  <ul class="kk-list">
    <li><strong>Google Business Profile, done right.</strong> The right categories, every service listed, real photos of real work, posts that keep it alive. Set up the way Google ranks it, not the way the sign-up wizard leaves it.</li>
    <li><strong>A review system.</strong> A text after every job, one tap, within Google's rules. Reviews keep coming without you remembering to ask.</li>
    <li><strong>Listings cleanup.</strong> Your name, phone, address, and hours matched on every directory that matters, so Google stops second-guessing you.</li>
    <li><strong>Area pages.</strong> A page for each city or part of town you want to win, linked to your profile, with what you actually do there.</li>
    <li><strong>Service-area setup.</strong> No storefront? The profile shows your service area instead of an address. That's how we rank ourselves.</li>
    <li><strong>Call tracking.</strong> Calls and clicks from the profile tracked, so you can see what the map brings in. Not a report full of charts. A number.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>THE HONEST PART</h2>
  <p>Local SEO is the cheapest high-impact work there is for a service business, and it's usually where we start. But it's not instant. Profile fixes show up in days. Reviews take weeks. Area pages take a couple of months.</p>
  <p>That's why we don't sell it on its own and tell you to wait. It runs inside Demand Flow next to the ads and the list, so the phone rings this month while the map catches up. The map is the part that keeps paying after the ad spend stops.</p>
  <p>And when somebody does call from the map, somebody picks up inside five minutes. That part's written into our guarantee, because the best map spot in town is worth nothing if the call goes to voicemail.</p>
  <p class="kk-ps">P.S. We rank on the map in El Paso as a service-area business with no storefront. Same playbook. It's in Marketing is F*cking Easy, free, further down.</p>
